  "environment_note": "TEST FIXTURE, NOT EVIDENCE. Copied from the monorepo manifest conductor/mutation_campaigns/claude_aria_kernels_mull_20260906.json so the adapter unit tests keep the exact shape (35 pinned sources, 202-entry survivor baseline, two executables) they were written against. The pinned aria_core paths do not exist in this repository and no test here reads them from disk. Two identical single-threaded-engine runs disagreed on five mutants, and the allocator was only half the story. MALLOC_PERTURB_ fills fresh and freed glibc allocations with a fixed byte, so a mutant that reads heap memory before writing it fails the same way every run -- that covers linalg.cpp:302, where narrowing a memset leaves part of the output matrix untouched. It did not settle the other four, all in math_space.cpp, because those kernels are '#pragma omp parallel for' and two of them reduce in floating point. Thread count and scheduling decide the summation order, the last bits of the result move, and a mutant whose damage is small lands on either side of the test tolerance depending on how the runtime happened to divide the loop. OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 with a static schedule fixes the order, which makes the verdict a property of the mutant instead of a property of the machine. Both settings make the instrument stricter, never more forgiving: they remove ways for a broken mutant to look alive by luck.",
